      <!-- Panel 1 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">01</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The camel you did not know you had.
        </h2>

        <p>
          For most of the last three million years, the American
          West was camel country.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Camelops hesternus</em> &mdash; "yesterday's camel"
          &mdash; was the dominant North American camelid of the
          late Pleistocene. It stood roughly 2.1 metres at the
          shoulder, somewhat larger than a modern dromedary, and
          carried an estimated body mass of around 800
          kilograms.<sup><a href="#fn1" id="ref1">1</a></sup> Its
          fossil record stretches from Beringia in the
          Yukon<sup><a href="#fn2" id="ref2">2</a></sup> through
          the Great Basin, the Columbia Plateau, the southern
          Rockies, the southwestern deserts, the southern Plains,
          and the California coast, with La Brea (Rancho La Brea)
          yielding one of the densest single-locality assemblages
          on the continent.<sup><a href="#fn3" id="ref3">3</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          Most modern readers carry a picture of camels as Old World
          animals &mdash; dromedaries and Bactrians. That picture is
          backwards. Camelidae evolved on the North American
          continent across most of the Cenozoic.<sup><a href="#fn4" id="ref4">4</a></sup>
          The ancestors of modern Old World camels crossed into
          Eurasia via Beringia; the ancestors of South American
          llamas and vicu&ntilde;as crossed south into what is now
          Peru and the Altiplano. <em>Camelops</em> stayed. It was
          a North American animal for the entire history of its
          genus.
        </p>

        <p>
          Genomic work published in 2015 revised the family tree
          further: <em>Camelops</em> is more closely related to
          living Old World camels (<em>Camelini</em>) than to any
          of the living New World llamas (<em>Lamini</em>), reversing
          a placement that had persisted in the morphological
          literature for decades.<sup><a href="#fn5" id="ref5">5</a></sup>
          The camels that turn up on the Silk Road are descendants
          of North American emigrants. <em>Camelops</em> is their
          closer kin than the llama is.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then, across the last several centuries of the Pleistocene,
          <em>Camelops</em> disappeared. The most robust synthesis
          of terminal-Pleistocene last-appearance dates places the
          final <em>Camelops</em> specimens at or before approximately
          13,000 calibrated years before present, consistent with
          the broader North American megafaunal
          collapse.<sup><a href="#fn6" id="ref6">6</a></sup>
        </p>

        <figure class="bg-bone/60 border border-ink/10 p-6 my-8">
          <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Component &bull; Phylogeny inset</div>
          <p class="font-serif text-sm text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
            Small cladogram. Camelidae split: <em>Camelini</em>
            (Old World, living), <em>Lamini</em> (South American,
            living), <em>Camelops</em> (North American, extinct),
            with <em>Camelops</em> placed as sister to
            <em>Camelini</em> per Heintzman et al. 2015.
          </p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="bg-bone/60 border border-ink/10 p-6 my-8">
          <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Component &bull; Body-size comparison</div>
          <p class="font-serif text-sm text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
            Silhouettes at scale: <em>Camelops hesternus</em>,
            dromedary, llama, human. Annotated with shoulder height
            and mass estimates.
          </p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- Panel 2 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">02</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What <em>Camelops</em> ate.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Direct dietary evidence for <em>Camelops</em> is unusually
          good for an extinct megaherbivore. Dung deposits recovered
          from dry caves &mdash; Gypsum Cave in Nevada and several
          southwestern sites &mdash; preserved the plant material of
          <em>Camelops</em> last meals and were subjected to
          systematic macrobotanical analysis by Akersten, Foppe,
          and Jefferson in 1988. The dung contained a mix of grasses,
          sedges, and woody browse, including yucca, desert shrubs,
          and members of the pea family.<sup><a href="#fn7" id="ref7">7</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          Subsequent dental microwear and mesowear studies support
          the same conclusion from a different angle: <em>Camelops</em>
          tooth wear patterns are consistent with a mixed feeder
          &mdash; part grazer, part browser &mdash; shifting toward
          more browsing in more arid and more shrub-dominated
          systems.<sup><a href="#fn8" id="ref8">8</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical implication for the landscape is the thing
          the page exists to make visible. <em>Camelops</em> was not
          a pure grazer competing with the bison and mammoths for
          grass. It was a mixed feeder that could crop mesquite,
          juniper, and shrub understory and move back and forth
          across grassland, shrubland, and the ecotones between. A
          vegetation community assembled around a mixed feeder of
          that size behaves differently, across decades, from one
          assembled without.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Panel 3 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">03</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The assemblage.
        </h2>

        <p>
          <em>Camelops</em> did not stand alone. The late Pleistocene
          American West was arranged around a set of large-bodied
          vertebrates that had been co-occurring, in overlapping and
          evolving assemblages, for hundreds of thousands of
          years.<sup><a href="#fn9" id="ref9">9</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          The partial list of what shared the continent with
          <em>Camelops</em> at 15,000 BP: mammoths
          (<em>Mammuthus columbi</em> across the southern continent,
          <em>Mammuthus primigenius</em> in the north); mastodons
          (<em>Mammut americanum</em>, principally in the forested
          east and the Pacific Northwest); horses (<em>Equus
          occidentalis</em> and several other <em>Equus</em> species
          ranging the basin and plains); another camelid
          (<em>Hemiauchenia macrocephala</em>, a larger-bodied
          llamine, a llama relative, not a close cousin of
          <em>Camelops</em>);<sup><a href="#fn10" id="ref10">10</a></sup>
          ground sloths (<em>Nothrotheriops shastensis</em>,
          <em>Paramylodon harlani</em>, <em>Megalonyx jeffersonii</em>);
          bison (<em>Bison latifrons</em> early in the interval,
          <em>Bison antiquus</em> later); and the predator guild
          (<em>Smilodon fatalis</em>, <em>Panthera atrox</em>,
          <em>Miracinonyx trumani</em>, <em>Arctodus simus</em>,
          <em>Canis dirus</em>).
        </p>

        <p>
          Almost all of these animals are gone. The bison survived,
          in a close-relative form. The horse lineage was extirpated
          on the continent and returned only with the Spanish in the
          1490s. Everything else on the list vanished.
        </p>

        <figure class="bg-bone/60 border border-ink/10 p-6 my-8">
          <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Component &bull; Assemblage chart</div>
          <p class="font-serif text-sm text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
            Horizontal timeline, 50,000 BP to present. Each species
            as a horizontal bar showing inferred range overlap;
            <em>Camelops</em> highlighted. Last-appearance dates
            with confidence intervals, sourced from PBDB and the
            Faith &amp; Surovell 2009 synthesis. Human-arrival
            boundary marked per current pre-Clovis and Clovis
            chronology.
          </p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- Panel 4 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">04</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          The fruits that are still waiting.
        </h2>

        <p>
          If you grew up in the American Midwest or mid-South, you
          have met an osage orange tree.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Maclura pomifera</em> &mdash; osage orange, bois d'arc,
          hedge apple &mdash; is common along old fence rows and in
          hedgerow plantings across the central United States. It
          was planted by the hundreds of thousands during the
          nineteenth century as a thorny living fence before barbed
          wire made it obsolete.<sup><a href="#fn11" id="ref11">11</a></sup>
          Its fruit is grapefruit-sized, grainy, faintly
          citrus-scented, bleeding white latex when cut. To any
          extant animal on the continent, that fruit is essentially
          inedible. Horses will nose one and walk on. Cattle refuse
          them. The fruits fall, rot in place, and stain the ground
          beneath the tree.
        </p>

        <p>
          In 1982, Daniel Janzen and Paul Martin published a paper
          in <em>Science</em> called "Neotropical Anachronisms: The
          Fruits the Gomphotheres Ate." They argued that a long list
          of New World plants produce fruits whose size, chemistry,
          and seed architecture are coherent only as signals to
          large-bodied mammalian dispersers that no longer exist
          &mdash; gomphotheres and other proboscideans, ground
          sloths, large camelids, giant
          tortoises.<sup><a href="#fn12" id="ref12">12</a></sup>
          Janzen and Martin named the condition ecological
          anachronism: a plant whose dispersal partner is out of
          its time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Within the American West and the transitional eastern
          edge of the <em>Camelops</em> range, the candidates are
          familiar. Osage orange (<em>Maclura pomifera</em>). Honey
          locust (<em>Gleditsia triacanthos</em>), whose long
          fibrous pods are sugary, indehiscent, and currently
          dispersed almost nowhere.<sup><a href="#fn13" id="ref13">13</a></sup>
          Kentucky coffeetree (<em>Gymnocladus dioicus</em>), whose
          heavy toxic pods have been reduced to a near-relict
          distribution in modern forests.<sup><a href="#fn14" id="ref14">14</a></sup>
          At the Pacific edge of the range, pawpaw
          (<em>Asimina triloba</em>), persimmon (<em>Diospyros
          virginiana</em>), and several <em>Prosopis</em> and
          <em>Acacia</em> species with comparable structural
          signatures.
        </p>

        <p>
          No single one of these trees proves the anachronism
          hypothesis on its own. Some of their fruits are partially
          vectored by surviving dispersers &mdash; white-tailed deer,
          raccoons, extant livestock, humans. The phylogenetic
          distance from the originally-hypothesized dispersers to
          modern ones is not always large enough to make the system
          fully dysfunctional. The argument is statistical: across
          the suite of New World plants that carry this set of
          traits, the ecological fit is with dispersers that no
          longer exist.
        </p>

        <p>
          The question the page asks is the one Janzen and Martin
          asked, restated for a visitor standing in front of a
          fallen osage orange on a Kansas fencerow: <em>what is
          this fruit for?</em>
        </p>

        <figure class="bg-bone/60 border border-ink/10 p-6 my-8">
          <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Component &bull; Plant&ndash;disperser pairing diagram</div>
          <p class="font-serif text-sm text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
            Two columns. Left: osage orange, honey locust, Kentucky
            coffeetree, pawpaw, persimmon, avocado. Right:
            <em>Camelops</em>, <em>Mammuthus columbi</em>,
            <em>Mammut americanum</em>, gomphotheres,
            <em>Megalonyx</em>, <em>Eremotherium</em>. Lines connect
            hypothesized historical pairings, each annotated with
            citation and confidence level.
          </p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- Panel 5 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">05</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          When near time ended.
        </h2>

        <p>
          "Near time" is the term the paleontologist Ross MacPhee
          and his colleagues introduced in 1999 to frame the last
          roughly 50,000 years of extinctions as a coherent
          scientific unit.<sup><a href="#fn15" id="ref15">15</a></sup>
          Within that window, North America lost more than forty
          genera of large-bodied mammals.<sup><a href="#fn16" id="ref16">16</a></sup>
          The <em>Camelops</em> lineage was one of them.
        </p>

        <p>
          The cause of the near-time extinctions has been argued,
          hard, for more than half a century. Three major hypotheses
          remain in play.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Human overkill.</strong> Proposed by Paul Martin
          in the 1960s and developed across his career, the overkill
          hypothesis holds that the arrival of skilled big-game
          hunters in North America at or near the beginning of the
          Clovis period (ca. 13,000 BP) produced the continental
          collapse via direct and sustained predation pressure on
          animals that had not co-evolved with human
          hunters.<sup><a href="#fn17" id="ref17">17</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Climate.</strong> Rapid climate oscillation during
          the terminal Pleistocene &mdash; most sharply at the onset
          of the Younger Dryas, around 12,900 BP &mdash; imposed
          substantial ecological stress on assemblages already at
          the edge of viability.<sup><a href="#fn18" id="ref18">18</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Cosmic impact.</strong> Firestone and colleagues
          (2007) and a subsequent body of work under the banner of
          the Younger Dryas Impact Hypothesis propose that a
          fragmented impactor event at approximately 12,800 BP
          contributed to the Younger Dryas cooling and accompanying
          ecosystem collapse.<sup><a href="#fn19" id="ref19">19</a></sup>
          The hypothesis remains actively debated in the
          peer-reviewed literature.<sup><a href="#fn20" id="ref20">20</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          The current scientific posture, reflected in the synthesis
          reviews, is that the extinctions were most probably
          multifactorial &mdash; some mixture of climate, human
          pressure, and habitat change, with the weight of each
          factor varying by species and by
          region.<sup><a href="#fn21" id="ref21">21</a></sup>
          David Meltzer's 2020 review in PNAS offers the most
          accessible recent synthesis of the critical case against
          a pure overkill account.<sup><a href="#fn22" id="ref22">22</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          The rewilding argument &mdash; which the book you are
          reading a companion site to is substantially about
          &mdash; does not depend on which cause prevails. What was
          lost was lost. The question of whether it can be partly
          restored is separate from the question of what drove it
          out.
        </p>

        <figure class="bg-bone/60 border border-ink/10 p-6 my-8">
          <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Component &bull; Extinction timeline</div>
          <p class="font-serif text-sm text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
            Horizontal timeline, 20,000 BP to 8,000 BP. Last-appearance
            dates for twelve North American megafaunal taxa,
            including <em>Camelops</em>, with confidence intervals.
            Overlays: human-arrival band (p3k14c); Younger Dryas
            boundary and termination; Bolling&ndash;Allerod / Younger
            Dryas temperature reconstruction from GISP2.
          </p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- Panel 6 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">06</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What stands here now.
        </h2>

        <figure class="bg-bone/60 border border-ink/10 p-6 my-4">
          <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Component &bull; Address lookup</div>
          <p class="font-serif text-sm text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
            Single input field: <em>Enter a ZIP code or address.</em>
            Returns the nearest documented <em>Camelops</em>
            occurrence, with locality, specimen catalog, estimated
            age, and distance. Source: PBDB geospatial query,
            rate-limited, cached. Nearby-flora inset lists any
            extant orphan-candidate species documented in the same
            county (USDA PLANTS, iNaturalist research-grade).
          </p>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The land <em>Camelops</em> walked is still there. Most
          of it is under a use pattern that has no memory of
          <em>Camelops</em> in it.
        </p>

        <p>
          In its former core range, the American West today is
          dominated by rangeland cattle operations, federal grazing
          leases, irrigated agriculture, and large blocks of
          protected federal land under the Bureau of Land Management,
          the Forest Service, and the National Park Service. Feral
          <em>Equus caballus</em> populations &mdash; the returned
          horse, the book's Chapter 2 case &mdash; persist in
          several western ranges, managed in practice by BLM
          round-ups.<sup><a href="#fn23" id="ref23">23</a></sup>
          Pronghorn (<em>Antilocapra americana</em>) remain on the
          plains and basins, still the fastest land mammal in the
          hemisphere. Bison remain in fragments: Yellowstone, the
          National Bison Range, a few state and tribal herds, and
          restoration projects that have been growing steadily
          across the last three
          decades.<sup><a href="#fn24" id="ref24">24</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          Osage orange, as noted, is everywhere and orphaned. Honey
          locust, cultivated and wild, is everywhere and orphaned.
          Kentucky coffeetree has contracted to a near-relict
          distribution, planted in cities for its ornamental
          qualities, barely dispersing in any wild
          system.<sup><a href="#fn25" id="ref25">25</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          The continent is, in the terms this site uses, operating
          at half volume.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Panel 7 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">07</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          What could stand here again.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Pleistocene rewilding as a formal scientific proposal
          dates to a pair of papers by C. Josh Donlan and colleagues
          published in <em>Nature</em> (2005) and <em>The American
          Naturalist</em> (2006).<sup><a href="#fn26" id="ref26">26</a></sup>
          The proposal, simplified: where close living analogues of
          extinct megafauna exist &mdash; and where ecological,
          regulatory, and social conditions make it possible &mdash;
          introduce those analogues as proxies for the missing
          species, at first in small and controlled settings, to
          test whether they can restore ecological function.
        </p>

        <p>
          Donlan and colleagues named candidate proxies explicitly.
          Bactrian and dromedary camels were among them, as proxies
          for <em>Camelops</em> and <em>Hemiauchenia</em>. Asian
          and African elephants were named as proxies for mammoths
          and mastodons. Bolson tortoises (<em>Gopherus
          flavomarginatus</em>) were named as a proxy for the extinct
          <em>Hesperotestudo</em> giant tortoises of the southwestern
          United States. The proposal was controversial when
          published, as its authors understood it would be, and it
          remains so. Chapter 9 of the book engages the critics in
          detail and fairly.<sup><a href="#fn27" id="ref27">27</a></sup>
        </p>

        <p>
          No current project in North America has introduced
          <em>Camelops</em> proxies at ecological scale. Several
          adjacent projects are doing work that would frame the
          possibility.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>American Prairie Reserve</strong> (Montana) is
          assembling a large contiguous prairie landscape around a
          restoring bison population, with the stated long-term aim
          of recreating a functionally complete grassland
          ecosystem.<sup><a href="#fn28" id="ref28">28</a></sup>
          The bison work is not proxy work &mdash; bison are
          themselves survivors &mdash; but the land base and
          governance model is the kind of scaffolding under which
          proxy experiments could be run, if political and
          regulatory conditions allowed.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The Bolson tortoise restoration</strong> at Ted
          Turner's Armendaris Ranch in New Mexico is the best
          existing North American example of a deliberate
          Pleistocene-proxy program at a working
          scale.<sup><a href="#fn29" id="ref29">29</a></sup>
          The tortoises were first reintroduced in 2006 and the
          herd has reproduced and persisted.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Pleistocene Park</strong> (Chersky, Siberia),
          under the direction of Sergey and Nikita Zimov, is not a
          North American project, but it is the fullest operational
          test of the underlying hypothesis &mdash; that reassembling
          a functional megaherbivore community changes the vegetation
          community and the soil carbon balance in measurable
          ways.<sup><a href="#fn30" id="ref30">30</a></sup>
          Their published data on soil temperature, vegetation
          change, and methane flux over two decades is the closest
          thing the field has to an experimental answer.
        </p>

        <figure class="bg-bone/60 border border-ink/10 p-6 my-8">
          <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-2">Component &bull; Project cards</div>
          <p class="font-serif text-sm text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">
            Filterable list of anchor rewilding projects, rendered
            here filtered to North American projects and to projects
            working on proxy restoration. Each card links to a full
            project page under <code>/one-hundred-camels/projects/</code>.
          </p>
        </figure>

        <p>
          No one has tried, at scale, what the Prologue names as its
          wish. The permits would be difficult. The ecological risk
          assessments would need to be rigorous. The neighbors would
          have to be persuaded. Some of the most serious critics of
          Pleistocene rewilding &mdash; Tim Caro, Dustin Rubenstein,
          and others &mdash; have argued that the social and
          political frictions alone are enough to keep the proposal
          from ever being realized at meaningful
          scale.<sup><a href="#fn31" id="ref31">31</a></sup>
          The critics are not dismissed here. Chapter 9 of the book
          engages them.
        </p>

        <p>
          What the page asks, and what the book asks, is narrower.
          Not <em>camels restored to the continent.</em> Room for
          the experiment. An osage orange orchard, a hundred camels,
          a study site, a research protocol, a multi-year data
          series on what happens when the fruit has a mouth again.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-10">
          <a
            href="/one-hundred-camels/the-experiment"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 bg-ink text-paper hover:bg-ochre transition-colors text-sm small-caps"
          >
            Read the full proposal
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Panel 8 -->
      <section>
        <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">08</div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mb-6">
          Read the Prologue.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The longing this page is built around is articulated in the
          book's Prologue, <em>One Hundred Camels.</em> It opens on
          <em>Camelops hesternus</em> and closes on the wish the
          page above is named for.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6">
          <a
            href="/the-book/excerpt#prologue"
