    <!-- Methods and data -->
    <hr class="hairline my-16" />

    <section class="text-sm text-ink/70 space-y-4 font-serif leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-4">Methods and data</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Fossil occurrences.</strong> <em>Camelops</em>
        locality data for the hero map and the address-lookup
        component are pulled at query time from the Paleobiology
        Database (PBDB) via its REST API, filtered to
        <code>taxon_name=Camelops, interval=Late Pleistocene</code>.
        Each occurrence carries its PBDB occurrence ID, its
        collection reference, and a link-through to the PBDB record.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Last-appearance dates.</strong> The extinction
        timeline uses the compilation in Faith &amp; Surovell (2009)
        with species-level updates from subsequent dating work
        (<em>Quaternary Science Reviews</em>, <em>Quaternary
        Research</em>). Refresh date shown at the foot of the
        component.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Flora distributions.</strong> Osage orange, honey
        locust, Kentucky coffeetree, pawpaw, and persimmon
        distributions from USDA PLANTS (public domain), supplemented
        with GBIF and iNaturalist research-grade records. Quarterly
        refresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Human-arrival chronology.</strong> Overlays use the
        p3k14c radiocarbon compilation and the pre-Clovis literature
        as engaged in Chapter 3 of the book. Shown as a range, not
        a date, because the arrival is genuinely uncertain.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Paleoclimate overlay.</strong> GISP2 ice-core
        temperature reconstruction. Sea-level / paleogeography from
        Lambeck et al. (2014).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A note on bias.</strong> Fossil localities reflect
        where paleontologists have dug, not where <em>Camelops</em>
        lived. Absence of dots on the hero map should not be read
        as absence of the species. The map is a map of the evidence,
        not of the Pleistocene.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Licensing.</strong> PBDB: CC-BY. Neotoma: CC-BY-4.0.
        GBIF: filtered to CC0 and CC-BY at query time. USDA PLANTS:
        public domain. iNaturalist: per-observation license honored.
      </p>
    </section>

